<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Humber</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Humber Estuary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Humber is a large tidal estuary on the east coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, formed by the meeting of the rivers Ouse and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> and flowing into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Since early times it has been a major boundary and routeway: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdom of Northumbria took its name from lying north of the Humber, and Hull and Grimsby grew as ports on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/humber.jpg" alt="Humber" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humber_Bridge.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
